  "finalAnswerPreview": "## Answer\\n\\nThe integration should expect **`InvalidCursorError`** from `@ordarium/core` — an `OrdariumError` subclass whose **`code` is `\\\"INVALID_CURSOR\\\"`** — thrown by `StateStore.changes()` / the underlying ledger's `changes()`. It must **not** expect the generic ledger failure recorded in the legacy register, and it must **not** be confused with `LEDGER_CORRUPT`.\\n\\n## What \\\"stored observation cursor\\\" means here\\n\\nThe observation surface is Ordarium's state change feed:\\n\\n- `StateChangeFilter` is documented as *\\\"Restrict observation to one namespace; absent observes every namespace\\\"* (`node_modules/@ordarium/core/dist/src/types.d.ts`).\\n- `StateChangePage.cursor` is *\\\"Opaque, filter-independent durable position of the last delivered change\\\"*, and unlike other pagination surfaces it is always present so a consumer keeps a durable resume point (`types.d.ts`).\\n- `StateStore.changes()` (`dist/src/state.js`) is the fail-closed entry: it requires `capabilities.stateChangeFeed === true` and delegates to `ledger.changes(filter, cursor)`.\\n\\n## When the cursor is rejected (two paths, same error)\\n\\nIn the pinned `@ordarium/ledger-sqlite` / `@ordarium/core` artifacts:\\n\\n1. *",
